Parallel worker lifecycle regression tests
==========================================

  timeout 180 ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_parallel_lifecycle]);'

Run each configured Lisp and require "No unexpected errors" in the log.
A zero exit status alone does not prove that Maxima had no Lisp errors.
The new file is included in the complete suite automatically.

The helper temporarily replaces the worker primitives in an otherwise idle
Maxima process. Real workers wait behind locked gates which only their join
wrapper releases. This deterministically detects returning before a worker
finishes: passing cannot depend on the scheduler finishing it early. A
10-second deadline and emergency cleanup prevent a failing regression from
leaving its own worker behind. The result is sampled before that cleanup.

The 19 examples cover success, failure of the first or second spawn, caller
errors and throws, one or multiple join errors, combined failures, empty and singleton jobs,
and the serial fallback. A private deterministic generator adds 64 cases
with different item counts, worker budgets and failure modes. Every started
worker must be joined, the budget must remain reserved while joining, and
its original value must return afterwards. The first caller error or throw
must survive a secondary join error. With normal caller return, the first
join error must be reported after attempts to join the remaining workers.

The helper restores replaced functions and diagnostic counters after each
case. CLISP exercises the applicable serial cases and skips native workers.
These tests cover ordinary Lisp error/throw unwinding. They do not establish
a cancellation policy for nonterminating computations or arbitrary external
thread termination; waiting for an existing worker still waits for its body.
